 "lines": [
  {
   "i": 0,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Before penicillin, an infected scratch could kill you. Then one scientist left a messy bench and went on holiday.",
   "from": 0,
   "to": 153
  },
  {
   "i": 1,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "London, nineteen twenty-eight. Alexander Fleming grows the germs that cause boils and blood poisoning.",
   "from": 156,
   "to": 269
  },
  {
   "i": 2,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "That August he goes on holiday, leaving his germ dishes on the bench, not in the warm incubator.",
   "from": 273,
   "to": 410
  },
  {
   "i": 3,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "September third, he's back.",
   "from": 414,
   "to": 448
  },
  {
   "i": 4,
   "speaker": "fleming",
   "text": "That's funny.",
   "from": 452,
   "to": 491
  },
  {
   "i": 5,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "A blue-green mould has landed on one dish. And all around it, the germs are dead.",
   "from": 494,
   "to": 616
  },
  {
   "i": 6,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "You see, the mould was making a chemical that kills bacteria. He called it penicillin.",
   "from": 632,
   "to": 758
  },
  {
   "i": 7,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "But Fleming couldn't turn it into a usable medicine.",
   "from": 761,
   "to": 818
  },
  {
   "i": 8,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Twelve years later, a team at Oxford finally did, in tiny amounts.",
   "from": 821,
   "to": 939
  },
  {
   "i": 9,
   "speaker": "oxford",
   "text": "It works!",
   "from": 943,
   "to": 965
  },
  {
   "i": 10,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Then American factories scaled it up. By D-Day, there were over two million doses.",
   "from": 969,
   "to": 1117
  },
  {
   "i": 11,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Now, what if he'd tidied them into the incubator?",
   "from": 1120,
   "to": 1179
  },
  {
   "i": 12,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "One expert argued the germs would have grown first, and the mould would have done nothing.",
   "from": 1183,
   "to": 1306
  },
  {
   "i": 13,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Maybe someone else finds it, but not in time for the war.",
   "from": 1310,
   "to": 1389
  },
  {
   "i": 14,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "By some estimates, penicillin and the antibiotics it inspired have saved hundreds of millions of lives.",
   "from": 1393,
   "to": 1542
  },
  {
   "i": 15,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Maybe someone in your family tree. All thanks to one messy bench.",
   "from": 1545,
   "to": 1646
  }
 ],
